  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nThe extreme heat will continue to spread across the FA this \nafternoon with dewpoints recovering well into the upper 70s to near \n80. Peak heat index values around 115 are progged while Tuesday will \nbe more variable with heat index values ranging from 105-115. For \nthis reason, southwest portions of the region are only under an\nExtreme Heat Warning for tomorrow with a Heat Advisory covering\nmore north and east. With that said, as a pre frontal shortwave\ntrough rounds a stout upper level ridge this evening, there is \na 30-40% chance of another round of scattered thunderstorms into\ntonight. The most recent runs of the CAMs show most the \ncoverage associated with a MCS remaining to the east, but the \nHRRR does hint at the potential for some development further \nwest. \n\nWhile the overall confidence in any coverage is low, any storms that \ndo develop have the potential to be highly impactful, as SPC \nmaintains up to a 15% chance of severe weather with damaging winds \nbeing the main concern. The areas of greatest concern is across \nnorthern portions of southern Illinois, all of southwest Indiana, \nand the Kentucky Pennyrile. MLCAPE peaks around 4000 J/kg this \nafternoon with 40+ kts of effective bulk shear, and a theta-e \ndifference of 30-40K. While the thermodynamics begin to wane \ntonight, they still remain sufficient enough for a severe risk \nbetween 7PM to Midnight. One final round of storms does move \nthrough Tuesday evening with rain chances peaking at about the \nsame as a cold front eventually moves through. Southeast \nMissouri, portions of southwest Illinois, and the Kentucky \nPurchase have the greatest risk. \n\nNear to slightly below normal temperatures follow through the \nremainder of the week with highs in the 80s as upper level northwest \nflow prevails across the Ohio Valley. Temperatures remain pretty \nsteady heading into next weekend, but daily rain chances quickly \nmake a return Friday as the GFS/CMC/EC all show a cut off low \ndeveloping in the vicinity that would result in a fairly unsettled \npattern.",
